0
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:03,250


1
00:00:03,250 --> 00:00:06,260
We've seen previously that if a rope is under tension

2
00:00:06,260 --> 00:00:08,590
and we approximate the rope is massless

3
00:00:08,590 --> 00:00:11,620
that the tension is then uniform everywhere along the rope,

4
00:00:11,620 --> 00:00:13,680
even if the rope is accelerated.

5
00:00:13,680 --> 00:00:17,370
However, if the rope has nonzero mass,

6
00:00:17,370 --> 00:00:20,610
then its tension will vary along its length.

7
00:00:20,610 --> 00:00:23,950
We can see that by looking at this example.

8
00:00:23,950 --> 00:00:34,400
Imagine we have a massive rope of length l suspended

9
00:00:34,400 --> 00:00:35,094
from a ceiling.

10
00:00:35,094 --> 00:00:38,280


11
00:00:38,280 --> 00:00:44,800
Now, it's easy to see that, at the top of the rope,

12
00:00:44,800 --> 00:00:47,070
a little element of rope right at the top

13
00:00:47,070 --> 00:00:50,720
has to support the entire weight of the entire rope.

14
00:00:50,720 --> 00:00:57,750
So if I were to examine just a little piece at the top here,

15
00:00:57,750 --> 00:01:01,640
the weight of the entire rope would be acting downwards.

16
00:01:01,640 --> 00:01:03,450
And for the rope to remain stationary,

17
00:01:03,450 --> 00:01:05,690
there has to be a tension upward.

18
00:01:05,690 --> 00:01:10,100
I'll call this t-top, because this is at the top of the rope.

19
00:01:10,100 --> 00:01:12,200
And so we see immediately from that

20
00:01:12,200 --> 00:01:14,780
that at the top of the rope, the tension

21
00:01:14,780 --> 00:01:19,720
is just equal to mg, where m is the mass of the entire rope.

22
00:01:19,720 --> 00:01:21,610
Similarly, if we asked what the tension is

23
00:01:21,610 --> 00:01:24,611
at the bottom of the rope, an element right

24
00:01:24,611 --> 00:01:26,860
at the bottom of the rope isn't supporting any weight,

25
00:01:26,860 --> 00:01:28,460
because there is no weight below it.

26
00:01:28,460 --> 00:01:36,100
And so at the bottom, the tension

27
00:01:36,100 --> 00:01:40,230
is 0, because there is no weight pulling

28
00:01:40,230 --> 00:01:41,880
on the bottom of the rope.

29
00:01:41,880 --> 00:01:45,700
So the tension is going to vary from mg at the top down to 0

30
00:01:45,700 --> 00:01:46,520
at the bottom.

31
00:01:46,520 --> 00:01:49,510
And if we wanted to work out at some distance

32
00:01:49,510 --> 00:01:53,370
from the ceiling-- let's call it x--

33
00:01:53,370 --> 00:01:56,660
at some position x-- say here, what the tension is

34
00:01:56,660 --> 00:01:59,610
at that point-- one way of figuring that out would just

35
00:01:59,610 --> 00:02:02,540
be imagine we cut the rope at this point

36
00:02:02,540 --> 00:02:05,140
and then asked what tension force

37
00:02:05,140 --> 00:02:09,254
would be necessary to support this bottom part of the rope.

38
00:02:09,254 --> 00:02:12,770


39
00:02:12,770 --> 00:02:16,280
This length is l minus x, so the mass

40
00:02:16,280 --> 00:02:23,010
of that fraction of the rope is l minus x over l.

41
00:02:23,010 --> 00:02:26,260
And the mass of that fraction of the rope

42
00:02:26,260 --> 00:02:29,010
is that fraction times m.

43
00:02:29,010 --> 00:02:33,980
And so the tension at this point, t at x,

44
00:02:33,980 --> 00:02:38,390
is equal to the weight of the length of rope

45
00:02:38,390 --> 00:02:39,260
below that point.

46
00:02:39,260 --> 00:02:42,980
So that's this mass times g.

47
00:02:42,980 --> 00:02:51,740
And I can rewrite that as 1 minus x over l times mg.

48
00:02:51,740 --> 00:02:55,750
And this gives me mg if I put in x equals 0,

49
00:02:55,750 --> 00:02:58,020
and it gives me 0 if I put in x equals l.

50
00:02:58,020 --> 00:03:00,360
So that's one way of figuring this out.

51
00:03:00,360 --> 00:03:02,670
I'd like to use this same example, though,

52
00:03:02,670 --> 00:03:07,590
to introduce another, more elegant way of analyzing

53
00:03:07,590 --> 00:03:10,260
what the tension as a function of a position on this rope.

54
00:03:10,260 --> 00:03:13,210
The advantage-- so this is going to be a little bit more

55
00:03:13,210 --> 00:03:15,780
complicated, but it's a much more powerful method,

56
00:03:15,780 --> 00:03:17,200
and it's one that we can generally

57
00:03:17,200 --> 00:03:20,540
use for any continuous distribution of mass as opposed

58
00:03:20,540 --> 00:03:24,030
to a point mass.

59
00:03:24,030 --> 00:03:27,675
So let's consider the same example again of a hanging mass

60
00:03:27,675 --> 00:03:29,820
of rope of length l and mass m.

61
00:03:29,820 --> 00:03:36,786


62
00:03:36,786 --> 00:03:41,462
Here's the length l and the mass m.

63
00:03:41,462 --> 00:03:42,920
And the approach we're going to use

64
00:03:42,920 --> 00:03:44,470
is called differential analysis.

65
00:03:44,470 --> 00:03:46,160
It's a technique from calculus, and it's

66
00:03:46,160 --> 00:03:48,706
applicable to any continuous distribution of mass.

67
00:03:48,706 --> 00:03:50,080
What we're going to do is imagine

68
00:03:50,080 --> 00:03:51,940
our continuous distribution of mass

69
00:03:51,940 --> 00:03:57,230
is made up of a whole bunch of little pieces, little elements,

70
00:03:57,230 --> 00:04:01,130
examine f equals ma acting on a single element,

71
00:04:01,130 --> 00:04:04,550
and then generalize to the entire mass.

72
00:04:04,550 --> 00:04:06,090
So let's do that here.

73
00:04:06,090 --> 00:04:15,680
What we'll do is we'll examine a piece at some position x.

74
00:04:15,680 --> 00:04:19,790
So I'm measuring x from the top.

75
00:04:19,790 --> 00:04:25,450
And let's say I define a little element here,

76
00:04:25,450 --> 00:04:32,430
which is that of position x and which has an extent delta x.

77
00:04:32,430 --> 00:04:34,560
So this thickness is delta x, and we'll

78
00:04:34,560 --> 00:04:39,880
call the mass of that little piece, that little element,

79
00:04:39,880 --> 00:04:41,281
delta m.

80
00:04:41,281 --> 00:04:43,280
Now, one thing I want to point out to begin with

81
00:04:43,280 --> 00:04:46,724
is that we want to pick an element that's somewhere

82
00:04:46,724 --> 00:04:48,140
in the middle of the distribution,

83
00:04:48,140 --> 00:04:49,360
not at one end or the other.

84
00:04:49,360 --> 00:04:51,430
The endpoints are special cases, so we

85
00:04:51,430 --> 00:04:53,370
want to pick a general case.

86
00:04:53,370 --> 00:04:56,300
So choose some x somewhere in the middle of our distribution

87
00:04:56,300 --> 00:04:57,480
which has a finite extent.

88
00:04:57,480 --> 00:04:59,110
That extent is delta x.

89
00:04:59,110 --> 00:05:01,460
In this case, we'll assume that that piece delta x

90
00:05:01,460 --> 00:05:02,940
has a mass delta m.

91
00:05:02,940 --> 00:05:05,720
So let's blow that up here and analyze it.

92
00:05:05,720 --> 00:05:08,985
So here is my element.

93
00:05:08,985 --> 00:05:11,650


94
00:05:11,650 --> 00:05:14,370
And it's going to have-- let's analyze what

95
00:05:14,370 --> 00:05:16,010
the forces acting on it are.

96
00:05:16,010 --> 00:05:19,370
So there's gravity acting downwards,

97
00:05:19,370 --> 00:05:24,390
which will be delta m times g.

98
00:05:24,390 --> 00:05:26,650
There is a tension acting upwards,

99
00:05:26,650 --> 00:05:28,795
exerted by the rope above it.

100
00:05:28,795 --> 00:05:32,150


101
00:05:32,150 --> 00:05:38,670
I'll write that as t of x, because it's at the location x.

102
00:05:38,670 --> 00:05:41,820
There's also a tension exerted in the downward direction

103
00:05:41,820 --> 00:05:44,900
by the remainder of the rope below the mass element.

104
00:05:44,900 --> 00:05:48,300


105
00:05:48,300 --> 00:05:52,190
And I'll call that t of x plus delta x.

106
00:05:52,190 --> 00:05:55,030
We expect the tension to vary along the rope.

107
00:05:55,030 --> 00:05:58,234
And because this element does have a finite extent,

108
00:05:58,234 --> 00:05:59,650
the tension at the bottom is going

109
00:05:59,650 --> 00:06:02,180
to be slightly different than the tension at the top-

110
00:06:02,180 --> 00:06:05,390
so t of x upwards, t of x plus delta x downwards,

111
00:06:05,390 --> 00:06:08,820
and then the weight downwards.

112
00:06:08,820 --> 00:06:11,210
So let's now-- that's our free body diagram.

113
00:06:11,210 --> 00:06:13,750
Let's write down Newton's second law,

114
00:06:13,750 --> 00:06:16,340
f equals ma for that mass element.

115
00:06:16,340 --> 00:06:21,180
So in the positive direction, which is downward,

116
00:06:21,180 --> 00:06:29,291
we have t of x plus delta x plus delta mg.

117
00:06:29,291 --> 00:06:31,040
And then in the upward or minus direction,

118
00:06:31,040 --> 00:06:34,180
we have minus t of x.

119
00:06:34,180 --> 00:06:36,610
So those are the combined forces.

120
00:06:36,610 --> 00:06:38,000
Now, this rope is suspended.

121
00:06:38,000 --> 00:06:39,420
It's not moving.

122
00:06:39,420 --> 00:06:43,530
And so mass object acceleration is just 0.

123
00:06:43,530 --> 00:06:45,770
I'm going to rearrange that.

124
00:06:45,770 --> 00:06:51,710
I can write that as t of x plus delta x minus t of x

125
00:06:51,710 --> 00:06:57,640
is equal to minus delta m times g.

126
00:06:57,640 --> 00:06:59,410
And by the way, let me remind you--

127
00:06:59,410 --> 00:07:04,447
if this were a massless rope, then delta n would be 0.

128
00:07:04,447 --> 00:07:06,030
And so the right-hand side would be 0,

129
00:07:06,030 --> 00:07:07,405
and the tension would be uniform.

130
00:07:07,405 --> 00:07:10,050
We'd have the same tension above and below.

131
00:07:10,050 --> 00:07:12,080
But because the rope does have mass,

132
00:07:12,080 --> 00:07:15,590
and in particular, this element has a nonzero mass delta m,

133
00:07:15,590 --> 00:07:17,830
there is a difference in the tensions.

134
00:07:17,830 --> 00:07:18,410
OK.

135
00:07:18,410 --> 00:07:23,890
Now, our delta m can be represented in terms

136
00:07:23,890 --> 00:07:25,450
of what this length is.

137
00:07:25,450 --> 00:07:33,130
Notice that that mass, delta m-- so note that delta m

138
00:07:33,130 --> 00:07:37,220
is just a fraction of the total mass

139
00:07:37,220 --> 00:07:38,960
that's in that particular mass element.

140
00:07:38,960 --> 00:07:42,210
Well, the fraction of the total rope

141
00:07:42,210 --> 00:07:46,980
is just the length of this element, delta x,

142
00:07:46,980 --> 00:07:49,830
divided by the length of the entire rope, which is l.

143
00:07:49,830 --> 00:07:51,780
So that's the fraction, and I multiply that

144
00:07:51,780 --> 00:07:53,270
by the total mass.

145
00:07:53,270 --> 00:07:57,660
So that is my mass delta m in terms of the length.

146
00:07:57,660 --> 00:08:01,420
So now I can rewrite this equation

147
00:08:01,420 --> 00:08:09,030
as t of x plus delta x minus t of x

148
00:08:09,030 --> 00:08:21,830
is equal to minus delta x m over l times g.

149
00:08:21,830 --> 00:08:25,870
Now, I'm going to rearrange this by dividing

150
00:08:25,870 --> 00:08:27,049
both sides by delta x.

151
00:08:27,049 --> 00:08:28,007
I'll do that over here.

152
00:08:28,007 --> 00:08:32,120


153
00:08:32,120 --> 00:08:40,409
So we have t of x plus delta x minus t

154
00:08:40,409 --> 00:08:49,380
of x divided by delta x is equal to minus mg over l.

155
00:08:49,380 --> 00:08:52,180
This tells us how the tension is varying

156
00:08:52,180 --> 00:08:57,890
over this small but finite-sized mass element, delta x.

157
00:08:57,890 --> 00:09:00,610
Now I'd like to examine what happens

158
00:09:00,610 --> 00:09:04,590
if I go to the limit of a small-massed element--

159
00:09:04,590 --> 00:09:08,440
the limit as delta x goes to 0, or in other words,

160
00:09:08,440 --> 00:09:10,980
the limit of an infinitesimally small mass element.

161
00:09:10,980 --> 00:09:17,040
So I'm going to take the limit of this equation

162
00:09:17,040 --> 00:09:19,720
as delta x approaches 0.

163
00:09:19,720 --> 00:09:22,370
Now, the left-hand side of this equation should look familiar.

164
00:09:22,370 --> 00:09:26,040
It's just an expression for the derivative of the tension

165
00:09:26,040 --> 00:09:28,380
t as a function of position.

166
00:09:28,380 --> 00:09:33,470
So I can write that as dt dx, and that's

167
00:09:33,470 --> 00:09:38,650
equal to minus mg over l.

168
00:09:38,650 --> 00:09:41,100
This is an example of a differential equation,

169
00:09:41,100 --> 00:09:43,669
or an equation that involves a derivative.

170
00:09:43,669 --> 00:09:45,210
This particular differential equation

171
00:09:45,210 --> 00:09:47,685
can be solved very simply by a technique called

172
00:09:47,685 --> 00:09:50,030
the separation of variables, where I just

173
00:09:50,030 --> 00:09:52,720
take each part of the integral-- the dt and the dx--

174
00:09:52,720 --> 00:09:55,440
and put it on different sides of the equation

175
00:09:55,440 --> 00:09:57,610
and then integrate both sides of the equation

176
00:09:57,610 --> 00:09:58,850
to find the solution.

177
00:09:58,850 --> 00:10:01,260
So in this case, I'll multiply both sides of the equation

178
00:10:01,260 --> 00:10:02,460
by dx.

179
00:10:02,460 --> 00:10:05,550
So I get dt on the left-hand side

180
00:10:05,550 --> 00:10:12,260
is equal to minus mg over l dx.

181
00:10:12,260 --> 00:10:14,640
And now I want to integrate both sides.

182
00:10:14,640 --> 00:10:17,790
So I'll integrate this side.

183
00:10:17,790 --> 00:10:19,450
And remember, mg over l is a constant,

184
00:10:19,450 --> 00:10:21,033
so I can keep it outside the integral.

185
00:10:21,033 --> 00:10:23,210
And I'll integrate this side.

186
00:10:23,210 --> 00:10:25,180
I'm going to do a definite integral

187
00:10:25,180 --> 00:10:28,670
over the continuous distribution that I'm studying.

188
00:10:28,670 --> 00:10:34,370
So on the right-hand side, I'm going to start at x equals 0

189
00:10:34,370 --> 00:10:37,466
and go to my position of interest, which is x.

190
00:10:37,466 --> 00:10:38,840
And to avoid confusion, I'm going

191
00:10:38,840 --> 00:10:40,680
to call the integration viable dx prime.

192
00:10:40,680 --> 00:10:42,280
This is a dummy variable.

193
00:10:42,280 --> 00:10:48,900
So x prime here represents all the values of position,

194
00:10:48,900 --> 00:10:54,030
ranging from my first endpoint 0 up to my other endpoint x.

195
00:10:54,030 --> 00:10:56,410
So x here represents a particular position,

196
00:10:56,410 --> 00:10:59,290
whereas dx prime is a placeholder

197
00:10:59,290 --> 00:11:01,330
for all the positions between the two

198
00:11:01,330 --> 00:11:04,820
endpoints in my infinite sum, which is an integral.

199
00:11:04,820 --> 00:11:06,440
So that's on the right-hand side.

200
00:11:06,440 --> 00:11:09,590
On the left-hand side, I'm integrating the tension t,

201
00:11:09,590 --> 00:11:11,130
with respect to the tension t.

202
00:11:11,130 --> 00:11:14,080
My limits need to correspond to the limits

203
00:11:14,080 --> 00:11:16,160
on the right-hand side integral.

204
00:11:16,160 --> 00:11:19,750
So on the right-hand side, my lower limit is at x equals 0.

205
00:11:19,750 --> 00:11:23,010
So on the left-hand side, I want to have

206
00:11:23,010 --> 00:11:26,400
my lower limit be the tension at that position x equals 0.

207
00:11:26,400 --> 00:11:29,990
So that's t of 0.

208
00:11:29,990 --> 00:11:32,880
And then the upper limit of the integral

209
00:11:32,880 --> 00:11:35,930
is the tension at the upper limit of the position integral,

210
00:11:35,930 --> 00:11:38,754
so that's t of x.

211
00:11:38,754 --> 00:11:40,170
And again, to avoid confusion, I'm

212
00:11:40,170 --> 00:11:42,909
actually going to call the integration variable dt prime.

213
00:11:42,909 --> 00:11:43,950
This is a dummy variable.

214
00:11:43,950 --> 00:11:46,360
It's a placeholder for all the values

215
00:11:46,360 --> 00:11:49,560
of tension from the tension at x equals 0

216
00:11:49,560 --> 00:11:53,860
to the tension at my position of interest x.

217
00:11:53,860 --> 00:11:56,370
And so this integral now tells me

218
00:11:56,370 --> 00:11:59,790
how the tension is varying in a continuous fashion

219
00:11:59,790 --> 00:12:02,190
along this continuous mass distribution.

220
00:12:02,190 --> 00:12:05,340
So now I can evaluate both integrals.

221
00:12:05,340 --> 00:12:06,750
On this side, I have the integral

222
00:12:06,750 --> 00:12:09,410
of a constant with respect to the tension.

223
00:12:09,410 --> 00:12:17,850
And so that's just going to give me t of x minus t of 0,

224
00:12:17,850 --> 00:12:22,700
and that's equal to minus mg over l.

225
00:12:22,700 --> 00:12:28,930
The interval of dx prime from 0 to x is just x.

226
00:12:28,930 --> 00:12:34,410
So this tells me how the tension changes from the endpoint

227
00:12:34,410 --> 00:12:37,810
to some arbitrary position x.

228
00:12:37,810 --> 00:12:41,240
If I want to actually solve for t of x,

229
00:12:41,240 --> 00:12:44,241
I need to specify what the tension is at the endpoint.

230
00:12:44,241 --> 00:12:46,240
But we know what the tension is at the endpoint.

231
00:12:46,240 --> 00:12:47,400
We found that earlier.

232
00:12:47,400 --> 00:12:51,960
We solved from the simple argument that at the endpoint,

233
00:12:51,960 --> 00:12:56,410
the tension here is just equal to the weight

234
00:12:56,410 --> 00:12:59,700
of the entire length of rope below that point.

235
00:12:59,700 --> 00:13:03,000


236
00:13:03,000 --> 00:13:08,550
So we know that t at x equals 0 is just equal to mg.

237
00:13:08,550 --> 00:13:11,570
And therefore, the tension at position

238
00:13:11,570 --> 00:13:15,800
x is just-- if I bring T of 0 to this side

239
00:13:15,800 --> 00:13:26,050
is just mg minus mg x over l.

240
00:13:26,050 --> 00:13:35,640
And so I can just write that as mg times 1 minus x over l.

241
00:13:35,640 --> 00:13:38,520
So that tells me what the tension

242
00:13:38,520 --> 00:13:40,460
is as a function of position.

243
00:13:40,460 --> 00:13:44,810
Note again that if I put in x equals 0, I just get mg.

244
00:13:44,810 --> 00:13:49,370
If I put in x equals l, I get the tension is 0.

245
00:13:49,370 --> 00:13:53,500
And for any point in between, we see that the tension varies

246
00:13:53,500 --> 00:13:56,890
smoothly between mg and 0.

247
00:13:56,890 --> 00:13:58,930
This is exactly the same result we found earlier

248
00:13:58,930 --> 00:14:02,500
by just cutting the rope at x and asking

249
00:14:02,500 --> 00:14:05,040
how do we balance the weight of the remaining

250
00:14:05,040 --> 00:14:07,030
rope below that point.

251
00:14:07,030 --> 00:14:08,690
But the advantage of this technique

252
00:14:08,690 --> 00:14:10,580
which is a little bit more complicated,

253
00:14:10,580 --> 00:14:13,630
is that it's a very powerful technique

254
00:14:13,630 --> 00:14:16,540
applicable to any continuous distribution of mass.

255
00:14:16,540 --> 00:14:21,690
So in this specific problem, if instead of the uniform density

256
00:14:21,690 --> 00:14:25,570
rope that we had here, imagine we had a clumpy rope where

257
00:14:25,570 --> 00:14:29,670
the mass of the rope wasn't distributed smoothly,

258
00:14:29,670 --> 00:14:33,730
but there were clumps, little parts of the rope that

259
00:14:33,730 --> 00:14:37,100
were heavier than others, and so the density varied

260
00:14:37,100 --> 00:14:38,841
with position along the rope.

261
